tools\pipeline\ledger.json:44390:   "note": "*** 2026-09-12, codex farm: the first brief was REFUSED by the codex cybersecurity filter; re-worded as plain mutation testing it answered (regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/codex_promote_20260912b.txt).  regress/xscale_inten_probe.py CREATED from the witness + 16 codex replacements (D_OP1, INTEN_WRITE_PRESERVES_ISTEER, BLOCKED reporting, stale-prompt and ignored-timeout fixes, RESULT/CONTROL lines) + D_CRM1_TMR0, the crm-isolation row codex said remained open (tmr0 <- 0x5A5A0000, TMRx_ENABLE clear).  29 rows, UNMEASURED.  The gate_arm.sh section is designed, NOT applied.  MANIFEST entry owed. ***\n\n*** PASS 2, 2026-09-11 (GPT-6 Astra, effort max): **A CLAUSE-(d) FLOOR BREACH IS PREDICTED, NOT YET\nMEASURED.**  THE ROUND IS NOT SHIPPABLE UNTIL IT IS BUILT AND SCORED. ***  Cited: regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/codex_pass2_20260911.txt\n\nVerdict: *\"The detector has holes: an opcode1-gated reinstatement escapes, and several recorded\nkills are misattributed. I found no reason to change the deletion itself.\"*\n\n**THE ESCAPING MUTANT (`M_OPCODE1`), inside the existing inten load arm, after the assignment:**\n    if (opcode1 != 0) { fatal(\"TODO: XScale read from inten?\\n\"); exit(1); }\n\nIt keeps the enclosing predicates and introduces a DIFFERENT one, which is exactly the clause-(d)\nform a PURE DELETION owes -- not the original death condition widened.  **The seat DECODED THE\nLITERAL CP6 INSTRUCTION WORDS in the probe** and found every tested inten access carries\n`opcode1 == 0`; the only nonzero-opcode1 instruction is `E_UNKNOWN` (`opcode1=3, crn=9, crm=2`),\nwhich never reaches the edited arm and dies through `unknown` anyway.  So it predicts **26/26, a\nTOTAL ESCAPE**, while a guest issuing an inten read with nonzero opcode1 would be killed -- so it is\nNOT an equivalent mutant.\n\n*** THAT 26/26 IS A PREDICTION WRITTEN IN THE MEASURED COLUMN'S CHAIR, AND THIS PROJECT HAS PAID\nFOR THAT FOUR TIMES.  IT IS RECORDED AS O