r.*\n\nTHE FIX ITSELF IS UNCHANGED AND ENDORSED: *\"I would keep the fix exactly as supplied\"*, and ACCEPT\n(option-set member 4) is upheld -- *\"I cannot justify a once-only announcement for the implemented\nread.\"*  **Eight passes on this round's detector, none on its fix until now, and the first pass on\nthe fix approved it while finding the detector's enumeration incomplete.**\n\n---\n\n*** 2026-09-11 L4 RECORD CORRECTION -- THIS ROW READ `state: open' WITH NO WITNESS FIELD WHILE A\nTWO-COLOUR RUNG-3 WITNESS, A PRETABLE.out AND A GREEN SEVEN-LEG KILL TABLE SAT IN ITS OWN\nARTIFACTS DIRECTORY. ***\n\nI wrote `xscale -- no round run' into the checkpoint this afternoon and it was FALSE.  Found by\nopening `regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/', not by any check.  What is measured there:\n\n    BASE       26/26  FAILING: NONE          M_OPCODE2  19/26  (7 D_* rows red)\n    HEAD       11/26  (15 rows red)          M_RD15     25/26  (RD15_SURVIVE)\n    M1         23/26  (3 ANCHOR rows red)    M_NREAD    19/26  (7 D_* rows red)\n    M5         25/26  (ANCHOR_EXC)           RESTORED   26/26  FAILING: NONE\n\ncited: regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/killtable_20260911/SCORES.txt.  **Floor clause (a) is met\n(HEAD red), every mutant is dead on its intended rows, and the restore verified BOTH parts --\nsource cmp-vs-SEC CLEAN and binary FRESH -- before clearing the sentinel.**\n\nTHE DENOMINATOR, because the first draft of this correction overstated the class and the count\nrefuted it: 169 ledger rows sit in work/held/open with zero entries, which is the NORMAL state of\na filed row.  Testing each for an artifacts dir holding a witness or killtable narrows it to\n**FOUR** -- ppcrldimimask, ppcrlwimiwidth, wdcaltdigin and this row -- and the other three are\nrecorded CORRECTLY, `in work' with a witness field naming the artifact.  *The records practice is\nholding; this row was the sole outlier, and a systemic claim would have been wrong.*\n\nSTILL OWED and NOT discharged by this edit: the round has had THREE seats (codex x2 2026-09-10,\nagy x1 2026-09-11) against a standing NINE-SEAT directive, and no panel dir cites it.  The fix is\nuncommitted (` M src/cpus/cpu_arm_coproc.c'), byte-identical in est/, p1=CLEAN p2=FRESH in all\nthree build trees.  **HELD for the panel under the owner's hold-everything ruling -- this edit\ncorrects the RECORD only and licenses nothing.**\n\n---\n\nFILED 2026-09-10 from the GPT-6 Astra exit audit (regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt), which ranked it I1/E1. VERIFIED LOCALLY by opening the file before filing.\n\nIn `arm_coproc_i80321_6', crm 0 / crn 0, the load arm reads:\n    cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten;\n    fatal(\"TODO: XScale read from inten?\\n\");\n    exit(1);\nThe destination register receives the modelled value and the process then dies.  The STORE arm immediately below writes the same field, and the i80321 interrupt controller consumes it, so the register is fully modelled in both directions.\n\n*** THE DECISIVE EVIDENCE IS THE SIBLING CASE IN THE SAME SWITCH, and it is one screen away: `case 4:' (i80321_isteer) has the IDENTICAL structure -- load arm assigns `cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isteer;', store arm writes it -- and carries NO fatal() and NO exit(). ***  So crn 0's kill is an OUTLIER inside its own switch rather than a policy, which is the upstream-residue pattern this project has now found several times: the author hit the exit he happened to reach while developing, and the sibling he did not.  That is a far stronger warrant than 'it is a host kill', and it is one grep away -- exactly what the option-set rule's sibling check is for.\n\nREACHABILITY: machine_iq80321.c installs it explicitly (`cpu->cd.arm.coproc[6] = arm_coproc_i80321_6;'), and Iyonix does the same.  This is not an unattached helper. Emulated firmware reading its own interrupt-enable register is the natural consumer, and the generic coprocessor dispatcher enforces no supervisor-only precondition of its own.\n\nDISPOSITION: option-set member 4 (ACCEPT) -- delete the two lines, keep the assignment, with NO latch, for the same reason as [[mapledmaaddr]]: the read is implemented and is not ignored, so an announce would ship a wrong record.  FAULT is unavailable here in any case: memory_rw.c delivers a failed-device exception for MIPS and M88K only, and this is ARM.  [CORRECTED 2026-10-18 at the row's closure (the closure checks of GPT-6 Astra and Grok 4.7, regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/close_20261018/, each finding it independently; verified at source): wrong, and reasoned from the wrong mechanism -- the bug file retracted its own copy of this sentence on 2026-09-11.  A coprocessor register transfer is not a device access and never reaches memory_rw.c, and ARM does deliver undefined-instruction exceptions: `arm_cdp()` in src/cpus/cpu_arm.c raises `ARM_EXCEPTION_UND`.  ACCEPT stands on its own warrant -- the read is implemented and is not ignored.]\n\nNOT MEASURED and no witness built; the rung-3 attempt is owed.  Note a witness needs a coprocessor read from an ARM guest, which is a different vehicle from the device probes this project mostly writes -- gate_arm.sh's 358 decode checks are the nearest existing machinery.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10: ACCEPT IS RATIFIED, BUT MY SIBLING WARRANT WAS OVERCLAIMED AND IS NARROWED HERE.  Verified locally before recording. ***\n\nI wrote that the adjacent `case 4:' (isteer) 'carries NO fatal() and NO exit()'.  **FALSE ABOUT THE WHOLE CASE.**  Its READ arm is quiet, as I said -- but its STORE arm assigns the field and then `exit(1)' on ANY NONZERO VALUE.\n\n**THE ASYMMETRY I RELIED ON SURVIVES, AND ONLY IN THE READ DIRECTION:** case 4 READS quietly while case 0 READS and dies, which is the outlier evidence.  In the WRITE direction the two swap over -- case 0 writes quietly, case 4 writes and dies on nonzero.  So the file has kills in both cases, on opposite halves, and the correct statement is that THE READ HALVES ARE ASYMMETRIC, not that one case is clean and the other is not.\n\nThe verdict stands with a narrower warrant: **ACCEPT for the ordinary interrupt-enable read into a general register.**  Its sibling's write-side kill is a SEPARATE defect on a separate arm and belongs to a separate row, not folded into this one.\n\n*** THE OTHER SEVEN EXITS IN THIS FILE ARE TRIAGED, 2026-09-10 (xscaleinten/codex_coproc_20260910.txt, ZERO cyber flags) -- AND MY WORKING HYPOTHESIS IS REFUTED. ***\n\nI asked which of the other seven share this row's shape, on the theory that 'the value is already computed and then discarded' would make several of them one cheap round.  **NONE OF THE OTHER SEVEN HAS THE SAME WARRANTED TWO-LINE ACCEPT REPAIR.**  That is a useful negative: it stops a round copying this disposition across the file, which is exactly what the option-set rule warns about when a precedent is transferred without checking each site.\n\n**AND THE CENSUS UNDERCOUNTED THE FILE: eight ACTIVE exits plus SIX COMMENTED-OUT ones.**  The commented six are inert and are not defects, but they are worth knowing about -- a round de-fatalising a live site sits beside six places where someone already made that call and left the corpse in.\n\nTHE STRUCTURE, which is what decides how the remaining seven split into rounds: three CP15 sites, four CP6, one CP14 -- or by SHAPE, five specific-case sites, TWO DEFAULT-FED ENDPOINTS and one ENTRY GUARD.  The two default-fed ones are the priorities: CP6's shared `unknown:' label is reached from THREE defaults (the outer crm switch and both nested crn switches) and CP14's from FOUR plus a separate opcode2 guard -- so one repair at each label covers many decode paths at once.\n\n*** ONE SITE IS NEITHER A CASE NOR A DEFAULT, which no other exit in this queue has been: CP15's `rd == ARM_PC' ENTRY GUARD, before the switch, in BOTH directions. ***  A refusal that fires before any dispatch is a different fix shape from every other member of this class, and it is ranked last for that reason rather than because it is unimportant.\n\n*** REACHABILITY ANSWERED **YES** 2026-09-10 (xscaleinten/codex_witness_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags), AND MY NEGATIVE PREMISE WAS FALSE.  This row now has a ratified disposition, a confirmed path, and a named vehicle -- it needs a measuring seat and nothing else. ***\n\n**INSTALLERS: exactly two, and both assign BEFORE the `!machine->prom_emulation' early return**, so disabling PROM emulation does not remove CP6 -- `machine_iq80321.c' (default CPU `80321_600_2') and `machine_iyonix.c' (`80321_600_B0'), each a direct `cpu->cd.arm.coproc[6] = ...'.  No other installer or caller exists.  **Selecting an XScale CPU is NOT enough**: generic ARM init installs CP15 and, for XScale, CP14 -- never CP6.  Note the setup receives the BOOTSTRAP cpu only.\n\n**PATH:** guest fetch -> `arm_instr_to_be_translated' -> condition-selected `arm_instr_mcr_mrc' -> `arm_mcr_mrc' -> `coproc[6]' -> this handler.  **No privilege or mode check anywhere in that chain** -- dispatch does not consult CPAR (stored and read through CP15, and the source says it is otherwise unused).  Cold init already selects SVC32 with IRQ/FIQ masked, so no privilege transition is needed.  Prerequisites are ordinary: the condition must pass, the fetch must succeed, ARM state not Thumb, and CP6 must be installed -- with a null entry `arm_mcr_mrc' prints a generic TODO and **its undefined-exception call and `exit(1)' are COMMENTED OUT**, so absence does not terminate.\n\n*** MY NEGATIVE PREMISE -- 'the machinery is store/load-oriented and nothing drives a coprocessor transfer' -- IS MEASURABLY FALSE. ***  FIVE existing probes already execute CP15 MRC/MCR on `cats': `footbridge_sites_probe.py', `fbpending_witness.py', `fbpending_drain_probe.py', `pit8253_latch_probe.py', and gate 14's `arm_flags_probe.py' on `testarm'.  **The directly reusable pattern is `footbridge_sites_probe.py'** -- launch a stock machine into the debugger, plant instructions in RAM, set pc, step, check disassembly, inspect registers -- pointed at IQ80321 or Iyonix instead of `cats'.  **No machine or device addition is required**, which is what the witness ladder demands.  No dedicated CP6 test exists yet.\n\n*** AN ORACLE TRAP THAT APPLIES FAR BEYOND THIS ROW: A PRINTED STEP DISASSEMBLY DOES NOT PROVE THE INSTRUCTION EXECUTED, OR EVEN THAT ITS CONDITION PASSED.  The engine prints the disassembly BEFORE executing. ***  Any probe here that treats a stepped disassembly line as evidence of execution is asserting something the engine does not promise.\n\n**A NAIVE `write W, read, expect W' HAS FOUR INDISTINGUISHABLE FAILURES:** always-zero (passes when `W' is zero); a stale value equal to `W'; a read that never executes (passes when the destination already holds `W' -- *especially easy if the write-source register is reused as the read destination*); and a program that never ran at all, when the inspected register or memory already holds the expected value.  **The discriminating form:** two DISTINCT NONZERO values written and read in succession, the read destination deliberately pre-set to a third value before each read, and SEPARATE write-source and read-destination registers.  Plus execution evidence -- intended address, decoded coprocessor and register fields, the destination TRANSITION, and continuation -- with missing observations failing rather than defaulting to zero.\n\n**AND THE ORACLE NEEDS CONTINUATION AND SILENCE, not just a value:** the CURRENT code already assigns the destination before terminating, so the assignment alone is not the outcome under test.  Unchanged source predicts the named diagnostic and exit status 1; the corrected behaviour requires the right value, continued execution, and NO diagnostic from this read arm.\n\n**THE SAME VEHICLE REACHES EVERY OTHER TERMINATION IN THIS FUNCTION** -- interrupt steering (`crn=4', write, nonzero), interrupt acknowledge (`crn=8', write, ANY value including zero), and the shared `unknown:' label (either direction, unsupported `crn' or `crm').  `opcode1' and `opcode2' are NOT validated, so changing them alone does not route a recognised selection to `unknown'.  *** EACH SITE NEEDS ITS OWN PROCESS INVOCATION, because the first `exit(1)' reached prevents observing any later one. ***  That groups the COVERAGE work without implying the repairs should be identical.\n\n**A COUNT IN MY OWN BRIEF WAS WRONG: `crm=1' has SIXTEEN implemented direction arms -- eight reads and eight writes -- not fourteen.**  All sixteen are locally quiet, but *** 'quiet' DOES NOT MEAN 'a plain storage assignment' ***: `tcr0' and `tcr1' INCREMENT before returning their values, and a `tisr' write clears selected bits and deasserts timer interrupts.  A sibling-parity argument resting on 'the timer arms are plain accessors' would be wrong.\n\n*** 2026-09-11: WITNESS DESIGNED (rung 3, `-E iq80321`, six invocations).  Two of my stated premises were WRONG and the seat corrected both; one architectural fact it established changes what the round may CLAIM. ***\n\n**THE DEBUGGER CANNOT REACH CP6 STATE AT ALL, so clause (f)'s anchor rule cannot be satisfied in its usual form here.**  `debugger_cmd_reg()` rejects any coprocessor number outside 0-3 -- coproc 6 cannot even be asked for; `arm_cpu_register_dump()` prints GPRs, cpsr, banked registers and the CP15 control block and **names no CP6 field anywhere**; and the settings tree carries only `pc`, `r0`-`r9`/`sl`/`fp`/`ip`/`sp`/`lr` and `cpsr`, so `print` cannot reach it either.\n\n> **The ONLY way to observe `cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten` is to make a guest MRC copy it into a GPR -- which is the instruction under test.  There is no channel OUTSIDE the decode under test.**  The three substitutes (the MCR store arm at `:315` as a different branch naming the same lvalue; the `unknown:` printf anchoring the ENCODING; the crm 1 `trr0` bank anchoring BANK SELECTION) are each real and **none is decode-independent.  The contract must say so rather than claim an anchor it does not have.**\n\nMY WRONG PREMISES, both corrected by reading: (1) I said five existing probes execute CP15 MRC/MCR **on `cats`** -- `arm_flags_probe.py` is `-E testarm`, and its CP15 word `0xEE070F9A` is an MCR that ARMS A COMBINER, not a register read.  The substance held; the machine attribution did not.  (2) I proposed `cats` as reachable -- it is not: generic ARM init installs only CP15/CP14, so `arm_mcr_mrc` takes its `else` branch, **prints and does NOT exit**, leaving the poison unchanged and the step line disassembling a perfectly good `mrc 6,...`.  *That state is pixel-identical to 'the instruction did not execute', which is why row A2 asserts the ABSENCE of `[ TODO: arm_mcr_mrc:` with `cp_num=6`.  Without it the two conflate.*\n\nTHE BLIND SPOT I FLAGGED IS ANSWERED BY A ROW, NOT A COMMENT.  **P2** asserts, from the step-PRE snapshot, that each subject destination still holds its POISON immediately before its own step; if any already holds the expected value the probe prints `VOID` for every value row and goes RED.  **X4** is its inequality companion: arm N (subject word replaced by a NOP) must NOT show `S1` in r3 -- if it does, something other than the instruction under test put it there and every value row is worthless.\n\nTHE DISASSEMBLY GAP IS CLOSED BY MEASUREMENT, NOT BY ARGUMENT.  `cpu_dyntrans.c:282-296` re-reads and disassembles the word, and executes it afterwards at `:325`; the print path never consults the condition code.  So **arm C** plants `0x0E103610` -- ONE NIBBLE from the subject word -- and the step line prints `mrceq 6,0,r3,cr0,cr0,0` while r3 must NOT change.  *One word, one nibble apart, opposite answers: that is the statement that a printed CP6 MRC and an executed one are different events.*\n\nENCODING, derived twice rather than recalled: `MRC p6,0,r3,c0,c0,0` = **0xEE103610**, from ARM ARM DDI 0406C.d A8.8.108 (`ddi0406.txt:25478`) AND from the emulator's own field extractor at `cpu_arm.c:2645-2651`.  The two agree field for field.  General form `0xEE100610 | (crn<<16) | (rd<<12) | crm`.\n\nSIX INVOCATIONS, and the rule is stated rather than assumed: **one per terminating site, plus one per mutually-exclusive content of the subject slot.**  Arms E (`unknown:` at `:419`) and S (isrc write at `:333`) terminate in BOTH builds, so neither can share a process with anything downstream; D and L terminate pre-fix only; N and C are the same slot with different words.  **S is the blast-radius control: if it ever goes green the deletion was not scoped to one arm, and that is a FAIL.**\n\nFOUR OF SIX ARMS MUST BE BYTE-IDENTICAL ACROSS THE TWO BUILDS -- itself a measured statement of the diff's blast radius.\n\n(d) NULL-REPAIR: the fix adds no predicate, so the mutants reroute existing ones.  `if (l_bit)` -> `if (l_bit && cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten)` is caught by **V0 alone**, the reset read taken while the field is still 0 -- which is why V0 is in the program and was not dropped for brevity.  A kill re-added on a narrow `rd` is caught by the five distinct subject `rd` values {3,4,5,6,9}, `rd` being a floor axis because it indexes the guarded body's own lvalue.\n\nDISCLOSED (clause (e)): predicates on `opcode1`/`opcode2` -- every word here has both at 0, and the body never TESTS them (it only passes them to the `unknown:` printf), so they are not floor axes here.  Also disclosed: interrupt DELIVERY is unreachable from a stepping probe (`debugger.c:800`, `:835-840` stop the host timer), so this is necessarily a REGISTER-TRANSFER detector, not a behaviour detector.  And the `isteer`-nonzero kill at `:324` is the one remaining `crm 0` kill this detector does not observe.\n\nDISPOSITION UNCHANGED AND STRENGTHENED.  `:311` already computes the correct value before killing, and `dev_i80321.c:445-446` aliases the field into the interrupt controller (`d->enable`), read at `:86`/`:92` -- so it is live state a consumer reads, and an *\"unimplemented; ignored\"* message would be false in product code.  The sibling grep agrees: within `crm 0` the `crn 4` READ at `:318` is already a silent accept and the `crm 1` bank is silent both ways, so silencing `crn 0` makes the file MORE internally consistent.\n\nNO MMU PROLOGUE AND NO ARM T, deliberately: a coprocessor move touches no memory, so the only access is the instruction fetch at VA 0x8000, which `arm_setup_initial_translation_table(cpu, 0x4000)` maps identically.  **The probe header must say this in those words** -- a reader who knows `footbridge_sites_probe.py` will look for its arm T and must not conclude it was forgotten.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 ADVERSARIAL PASS 1.  **THE ANCHOR ADMISSION IS HALF FALSE, AND FINDING THE MISSED CHANNEL CONVERTS A DISCLOSED WEAKNESS INTO A CLOSED ONE.**  One FLOOR BREACH (L2) and one TOTAL ESCAPE were found; all seven encodings verified correct. ***\n\n**THE DEBUGGER HALF IS CONFIRMED, line by line:** `debugger_cmds.c:1662` rejects any coprocessor number outside 0-3 (`reg ,6` is refused); `cpu_arm.c:193-198` registers only `pc`, the GPRs and `cpsr`; `arm_cpu_register_dump` names no CP6 field; and `i80321_inten` appears in exactly three places in `src/` -- `cpu_arm_coproc.c:311,315`, `dev_i80321.c:446`, `cpu_arm.h:212`.  `DEVICE_ACCESS(i80321)` never touches `d->enable`, so there is **no MMIO alias**.\n\n*** BUT \"NO CHANNEL OUTSIDE THE DECODE UNDER TEST\" IS FALSE. ***\n\n    dev_i80321.c:446    d->enable = &cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten;\n    dev_i80321.c:84-87  { *d->status |= linemask;\n                          if (*d->status & *d->enable) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq); }\n\n**`i80321_assert()` is a SECOND READER of the exact cell, outside the CP6 decode, and its observable is the ARM IRQ line.  That is clause (f)'s \"side effect\" anchor, verbatim** -- and it is reachable SYNCHRONOUSLY inside one guest store: `machine_iq80321.c:61-64` wires the ns16550 as `irq=...i80321.28`, and `DEVICE_ACCESS(ns16550)` calls `dev_ns16550_tick()` inside the guest's own store for `com_ier` and `com_mcr`.  So `strb 0x02 -> 0xfe800001` then `strb 0x08 -> 0xfe800004` asserts line 28 and the `isrc & inten` test fires.\n\n**THE COST, stated honestly: the anchor arm must FREE-RUN.**  `cpu_dyntrans.c:205-209` suppresses the exception under `single_step`, and `irq_asserted` is in neither the `reg` dump nor the settings tree, so a stepping arm cannot see it.  Use the project's existing free-running shape: clear CPSR I, plant `b .`, breakpoint at **0x18** (`ARM_EXCEPTION_IRQ = 6`, and `ARM_CONTROL_V` is not in the reset set), `continue`.  Contrast row: `inten = 0` gives no exception and the `b .` spins to the stall budget.\n\n=== M1 -- A FLOOR BREACH (L2), AND IT IS THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION I MOST WANTED ===\n\n    if (l_bit) { r[rd] = inten;\n                 if (cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isrc != 0) { fatal(...); exit(1); } }\n\n**`i80321_isrc` is 0 in every one of the six arms** -- nothing asserts an i80321 interrupt in a bare probe, and the only guest-reachable writer of `isrc` is the crn-8 MCR, which `exit(1)`s.  **On a real guest it is NEVER 0 at the moment INTCTL is read, because NetBSD reads INTCTL from inside the interrupt path.**  The host dies exactly as before and nothing in the six arms can see it.\n\nGraded L2 rather than clause-(e) debt under the pass-11 generating-set ruling: `inten`'s ONLY consumer is `i80321_assert`/`i80321_deassert`, which reads `*d->status & *d->enable`, so `isrc` and `inten` are a JOINT INPUT of the model, written by different paths.  The floor is `{l_bit} x {inten classes} x {isrc = 0, != 0}` and **the whole `isrc != 0` column is empty.**  *The same anchor arm closes it: asserting the serial gives `isrc = 1<<28`, readable back through `MRC p6,0,Rt,c8,c0,0` = `0xEE187610` -- the crn-8 LOAD has no `exit(1)` (`:328-329`), a fact the design did not use.*\n\n=== M5 -- A TOTAL ESCAPE, AND IT IS NOT A HOST KILL, WHICH MAKES IT WORSE ===\n\nSwap the cell on BOTH sides to `i80321_isteer`: reset read 0, three round-trips, L, N, C, E and S all GREEN -- **while the device's interrupt enable mask is never written and a guest's interrupts silently never fire.**  The design's own anchor admission conceded this was invisible; with the channel above found, it is closable.\n\n=== THREE MORE ESCAPES, EACH CLOSED BY ONE WORD ===\n* **M2 -- the detector's real bound is FOUR READS PER PROCESS.**  Arm D performs exactly four subject reads and L performs one, each in a fresh process, so a `static int nread; if (++nread > 4) exit(1);` escapes 6/6 while a guest polling INTCTL dies.  Repair: one more read in arm D.\n* **M3 -- the `rd` axis is not closed, and the manual says WHICH value matters.**  DDI 0406 A8.8.108: *\"`<Rt>` ... can be R0-R14 or, in some cases, APSR_nzcv\"* -- **Rt = R15 means write the CONDITION FLAGS**, and GXemul writes `r[15]`, the PC, unconditionally.  That is a real modelling defect, so a future `if (rd == ARM_PC) exit(1)` is likely.  `{3,4,5,6,9}` is five of sixteen and **omits the one value with an architectural special case**.  `0xEE10F610`.\n* **M4 -- `opcode1`/`opcode2` are ignored by the arm and CONSTANT across all six invocations.**  `if (opcode2 != 0) exit(1)` escapes 6/6 and re-opens the kill for 7/8 of the encoding space.  Close with `0xEE10A630`.\n\n=== CORRECTIONS TO THE DESIGN ===\n* **A FOURTH TERMINATING SITE IS UNROWED**: crn 4 STORE with a non-zero value (`:320-325`).  It is the subject's nearest sibling inside the same `case crm 0:` switch, and under SIBLING PARITY crn 4 is uncovered in BOTH directions -- including the silent-accept read at `:317-318` that the disposition argument leans on.\n* **L, N and C MERGE into one invocation** -- the rule binds only if the words must occupy the same address, and nothing requires that.  Six arms -> four, before the additions.\n* **Arm E is strengthened for free**: `0xEE107612` yields three ZERO fields, so a broken extractor still mostly matches.  **`0xEE79B6B2`** gives `opcode1=3 opcode2=5 crn=9 crm=2 rd=11 l=1` -- six distinct non-trivial fields, turning E into a decode-field anchor at zero cost.\n* **THE NOP WAS NAMED, NOT ENCODED.**  *\"the NOP (`mov r0,r0`)\"* is a LABEL; the word is `0xE1A00000`.  That is precisely this project's hand-assembled-encoding trap, recorded four times.\n* **MY NON-INTERFERENCE REASON WAS WRONG AND THE CONCLUSION SURVIVES.**  I said the tick cannot fire inside a `step` because its input is a `timer_add` callback.  **The tick DOES fire** -- `machine.c:354` initialises `ticks_till_next[n] = 0` and `:727-730` fires at `<= 0`, so every tick function runs on step 1 of every invocation.  What protects the cell is the ELSE branch: `tmr0` is 0 at reset, so the else writes `i80321_isrc` and `tisr` and never `inten`.  *This matters for the free-running anchor arm, which must not assume the tick is inert -- that same step-1 tick calls `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT` whenever `!(isrc & inten)`.*\n\n=== VERIFIED SOUND ===  All seven encodings confirmed against BOTH DDI 0406 A8.8.108/A8.8.99 and the emulator's own extractor, field for field, with three near-miss combiner traps cleared (`mia*`, `0xee070f9a`, `0x8afffffa`).  VA 0x8000 verified and **more robust than claimed** -- it holds even under `-Q`, because with the MMU off VA == PA.  *But the margin is ZERO: the L1 table ends at 0x7FFF and the code starts at 0x8000, so any literal pool below the code lands in the table.*  Disposition confirmed as option-set member **4 ACCEPT**, the DC21285 `+0x188` precedent exactly.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- WITNESS BUILT AND MEASURED (agy 3.8, scratch clone).  **THE ANCHOR ARM IS THE ONLY THING THAT KILLS M1 AND M5, MEASURED.** ***\n\n    witness, committed HEAD   SCORE 11/26   (15 rows red)\n    witness, with the fix     SCORE 26/26\n    BASE 26/26   RESTORED 26/26   restore verified BOTH parts (cmp CLEAN, binary FRESH)\n\n**THE KILL TABLE VINDICATES THE ADVERSARIAL PASS BY MEASUREMENT, which is the whole point of having built the anchor arm:**\n\n    M1        23/26   FAILING: ANCHOR_M1, ANCHOR_EXC, ANCHOR_CONTRAST\n    M5        25/26   FAILING: ANCHOR_EXC\n    M_OPCODE2 19/26   M_RD15 25/26   M_NREAD 19/26\n\n> **`M1` -- the floor breach -- dies on NOTHING BUT THE THREE ANCHOR ROWS.  `M5` -- the total escape that is not a host kill -- dies on EXACTLY ONE ROW, `ANCHOR_EXC`.**  Every stepping arm is green under both.  *So without the free-running anchor arm the six-arm design would have shipped with a floor breach and a total escape, exactly as the review predicted from reading alone.*\n\nThat is this project's *a reading seat buys the CLASS, a measuring seat buys the INSTANCE* rule with both halves present in one day: the review named `M1` and `M5` from the source and said the stepping arms could not see them; the table shows they are seen by three rows and one row respectively, and by nothing else.\n\n`M_OPCODE2` and `M_NREAD` each red the same seven D-arm rows (19/26), and `M_RD15` reds exactly `RD15_SURVIVE` (25/26) -- so the three cheap one-word additions the review prescribed each carry a measured kill rather than being precautionary.\n\n**ORDER: the PRETABLE discriminator HOLDS** -- `PRETABLE.out` at `02:18:05`, `build_BASE.log` at `02:20:51`, so the block was written 2m46s before table 1 and its own mtime proves it.\n\n*** BUT ONE ORDER CLAIM IS NOT EVIDENCED AND I WILL NOT ASSERT IT: whether the COMMITTED witness leg was run BEFORE the edit was applied. ***  `witness_fix.out` carries mtime `02:20:10` and `witness_committed.out` `02:20:23` -- **the fix leg is the EARLIER file** -- and the report does not address ordering anywhere (grep for order/test-first/before-any-edit returns nothing).\n\n**What IS established is weaker but real: the kill table's own `HEAD` leg checks out the committed source, rebuilds and probes, scoring 11/26.**  So a pre-fix measurement exists and is reproducible; what is missing is evidence that it was taken FIRST, which is what witness-ladder clause (i) asks for.  *This project's oracle-correction rule exists because a measurement taken after the edit cannot be distinguished from one fitted to it -- and the two mtimes here are the only thing that speaks to the question, and they point the wrong way.*\n\n**OWED: either a statement from the run that the committed leg preceded the edit, or a re-run of the committed leg from a clean checkout with its mtime recorded.**  Cheap either way, and it is the difference between a witness and a detector in this project's own vocabulary.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PROPAGATION CHECK (two-part clearance, RUN not asserted).  **ITS SEC EDIT IS IN NO BUILD TREE, AND ALL THREE BINARIES REPORT `FRESH`.** ***\n\n`cpu_arm_coproc.c` carries this row's uncommitted SEC edit (2 lines removed).  The clearance was run against all three build trees and `est/`:\n\n    tree               part 1 (cmp vs SEC)   part 2 [binary -nt source]\n    build/             DIVERGENT             FRESH   (binary 00:48:13)\n    build-asan/        DIVERGENT             FRESH   (binary 00:51:01)\n    /tmp/gxsec-build   DIVERGENT             FRESH   (binary 00:51:42)\n    est/               SOURCE tree -- no binary exists, part 2 N/A (inapplicable, not unrun)\n\n*The other three edited files in the same working tree -- `cpu_ppc_instr.c`, `dev_lca.c`, `dev_sgi_re.c` -- are CLEAN in all three, so this is a single missed propagation rather than a tree-wide drift.*\n\n> *** THE DANGEROUS PART IS THAT PART 2 GIVES NO WARNING: all three binaries are NEWER than their sources, so `FRESH` is TRUE and MEANINGLESS -- the binary is newer than a source that is simply the wrong source. ***  **Part 2 tests staleness against whatever source sits in the tree; only part 1 tests whether that is the source you think it is.**  A round that ran only the freshness half here would measure PRE-FIX ARM coprocessor behaviour and see nothing wrong.\n\n**None of the four files is in the divergence set** (derived, 6 paths), so the clearance genuinely applies and `DIVERGENT` here means UNPROPAGATED rather than by-design.\n\n*** NOT PROPAGATED IN THIS PASS, DELIBERATELY. ***  Propagating without rebuilding is the trap this project has paid for twice, and a build was in flight (agy, `wdcaltdigin` witness).  **Propagate and rebuild together, then re-run BOTH parts** -- recorded in `_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md` with the restore recipe so a fresh session finds it.\n\n*Found by running the clearance at a round boundary rather than at a mutation boundary -- which is the carrier's own finding that the two-part clearance is a ROUND-BOUNDARY obligation and that the sentinel discipline cannot catch this class at all, because nothing mutated anything.*",
